#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# point

#% text
# `point([x, y])` is a graphics primitive: a point at [x, y].

#% text
# `point([[x1, y1], [x2, y2], ...])` is several points.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Points are drawn as dots in the current colour; `point_size` (a fraction of the plot width) or `absolute_point_size` (in points) sets their diameter.

#% item
# A position may also be `scaled([u, v])`, a fraction of the plot range.

#% item
# A packed array of n pairs (as `table` makes) is read without unpacking, so large point sets are cheap.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# One point:

#% cell
graphics(point([1, 2]))
#% output
#= image("point.files/point-1.png")

#% text
# Three larger red points:

#% cell
graphics([red, absolute_point_size(8), point([[0, 0], [1, 1], [2, 0]])])
#% output
#= image("point.files/point-2.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Points of a computed list, with a frame:

#% cell
graphics(point(table([k, sin(k)], k, 3)), frame: true)
#% output
#= image("point.files/point-3.png")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `point_size`, `absolute_point_size`, `line`, `list_plot`, `graphics`
